A||||12||Alex2004||How do I install a 3rd brake light on an 84 SE?||||5||
Z||000000||Alex2004||12-25-2000||07:17 PM||||Hey, i have an 84SE and i want to install a 3rd brakelight. I have all of the parts but what all do i need to do to get this to work? Let me know, Thanks!<P>Alex.<BR> <A HREF="mailto:Alex917@excite.com">Alex917@excite.com</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||12-26-2000||06:58 PM||||I assume you have the OE Fiero 3rd brake light housing, and have figured out how to mount it to the top of the back window.  I also assume that the brake light wiring was essentially unchanged in the Fiero from 1984-1986.  Now the question is, which wire to tap into, to power your new light.  Correct?  If so....<P>Remove the lower cover from under the instrument panel on the driver's side (7mm socket, 4 screws).  You should be able to stick your head under there and see the brake light switch, above the brake pedal.  The switch has 3 wires - top to bottom, they are: light blue/ orange/ white.  Tap into the <B>blue</B> wire, the one on top.  Other side of the brake light bulb should be connected to a ground, of course.      <P>Double check - hook a 12-volt test light into the blue wire.  Operate the brake pedal, turn signals, hazard flashers.  If the test light behaves like you want the new 3rd light to act, you're okay.  The 3rd brake light should <B>not</B> flash when you operate the hazard flasher.  <P>Obviously, you'll want to take some care routing the new wire (from the blue on the brake switch, to the new 3rd light).  If you are careless and allow the wire to short (due to chafing, etc.), it'll blow the fuse and you'll be without brake lights!  <B>Not</B> good.  Find time to do it right, if you're going to do it at all.  In fact, you might consider adding a fuse where the new wire comes off the brake light switch, just to be safe.<BR>        ||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||12-26-2000||09:11 PM||||go by the colors, not physical location. The switch can be in diferant positions depending on its exact adjustment.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex2004||12-26-2000||10:07 PM||||Thanks for the info!!! Whenever i do that i will probably solder the wires.  All i really need to get now is the light wires.  Thanks again...<P>Alex...||||reg||4
Z||000004||Alex2004||12-26-2000||10:44 PM||||I just went outside and looked at the brake switch, umm....i have 4 wires. I have a white, orange, light purple, & a pink w/ black stripe. I tapped into the purple to see but nothing happend. Oh, and all of them are on top. So, what should i do?||||reg||5
Z||000005||StuGood||12-27-2000||12:31 AM||||Sounds like you have the wrong switch.<P><I>Stu was wrong!! - You had the right switch - found it in the 1986 Service manual, "Stop lamp & TCC Switch," it's two switches in one. The Pink/Black & Purple wires turn off the TCC when you hit the brakes.  The Orange & White wires power the brake lights - orange wire is always "hot." and the white one goes on to power the brake lights. </I><P>Let me guess - yours is an automatic?<P>Let us know what happens - I'm learning things here, too   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!  <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||SloPonti||12-27-2000||12:32 AM||||i did this in my 84 coupe and i used the white wire! this made the third brake light come on when i hit the brakes! <BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/sloponti/fiero1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||12-27-2000||12:58 AM||||Well, there you have it!<P>I'm curious, though, SloPonti - does the 3rd light flash when you turn on the hazard flashers?  Not that that's a bad thing, but now you've got me wondering   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P><I>BTW, I just edited my previous post - sorry about the confusion.  Didn't mean to lead you astray  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> !</I><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||10
